    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d348">

                <pb facs="0408" n="324" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg324"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 811.54262/152</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 7128</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-09-21">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 21, 1917</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received October 23.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    telegram No. 4742 of April 21, 1917,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d348-f1"/> requesting
                    information as to whether the British Government had made any arrangements with
                    enemy countries in order to secure to British subjects the right to communicate
                    with enemy subjects and to take the necessary steps in connection with the
                    filing of patent applications and the payment of patent fees, I have the honor
                    to enclose herewith a copy of a note I have received from the Foreign Office,
                    together with its enclosures,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d348-f2"/> in reply to the
                    enquiry I made in the premises on the 23d of April last.</p>
                <p>For the guidance of the Department, I venture to add that the contents of Mr.
                        Balfour’s note of July 10, 1917, to
                    which reference is made, were communicated to the Department by my telegram No.
                    6705 of July 11, 12 m.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>(For the Ambassador) <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edward Bell</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d348-a1.xml" xpointer="d348-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
